@@ -26,6 +26,45 @@
 
 mod_perl is currently considered stable.
 
+XPerl builds use hv_ksplit() to reserve hash storage and hv_fetch_ent()
+to look up internal entries. XPerl's hash representation does not support
+assigning to HvMAX or traversing HeNEXT bucket chains. These adaptations
+are conditional on XPERL_VERSION and preserve the ordinary Perl paths.
+
+On Perl builds with PERL_RC_STACK, direct Apache callbacks use a separate
+non-reference-counted Perl stack. Their traditional PUSHs/POPs operations
+borrow arguments from configuration objects and argument arrays. Unlike
+XSUB calls, these entry points have no automatic XS stack wrapper.
+Configuration, request and authentication callbacks must pair stack entry
+and exit around their temporary scope. Builds
+without PERL_RC_STACK keep their existing callback paths. This does not
+depend on the NV representation or require changing interpreter options.
+
+A DSO build requires a Perl embedding library built with position-independent
+code on platforms that require it. This applies to XPerl too. See the
+"64 bit Linux" section in docs/user/install/install.pod. Preserve the
+interpreter's thread, debugging, PERL_RC_STACK and numeric build options
+when rebuilding its embedding library.
+
+Apache-Test falls back to apxs SYSCONFDIR when neither the inherited
+ServerRoot nor apxs PREFIX is available. This resolves relative module
+includes on Debian and Ubuntu, where an unresolved mods-enabled include
+can prevent the test server from loading its MPM. The fallback is adapted
+from Niko Tyni's Debian 030-apxs-no-prefix.patch:
+https://sources.debian.org/patches/libapache2-mod-perl2/2.0.12-1/030-apxs-no-prefix.patch/
+
+The Apache 2.4 access test guards AuthUserFile and AuthGroupFile with
+mod_authn_file and mod_authz_groupfile respectively. Loading mod_auth_basic
+does not imply either file provider is loaded. The test's Perl handlers
+provide authentication and group authorization, so these optional file
+providers need not be enabled in the system Apache configuration.
+
+The runtime exception test identifies APR failures by their APR::Error
+class. Perl errors can be strings or objects: XPerl returns an
+XPerl::Exception for an undefined subroutine. A reference in $@ alone
+does not identify an APR error. Test the exception class when distinguishing
+APR failures from other errors, and retain the check for a caught error.
+
 The following test failures are known (see CPAN RT #118919):
 
   t/filter/in_bbs_inject_header.t (Fails tests 22, 26 and 30)
